From: Shouryamaan Jain <shouryamaanjain@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 20:25:29 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] feat(core): add session_history_search and
 session_history_read tools

Let the model recover an earlier user/assistant turn of this project, for
example one compaction dropped from context, without loading whole
transcripts. Both tools read Codex's own saved rollouts (CODEX_HOME/sessions)
through codex-rollout's listing and loading code and never write to them.

- Scope: saved root sessions whose recorded cwd is this session's cwd (the
  scope `codex resume` uses), plus this session. Model arguments cannot widen
  it. Hits report session_relation "current" or "other".
- A turn is a real user message, the final assistant reply, and the tool
  calls, result previews and apply_patch files up to the next user message.
  Compaction summaries and compacted checkpoints are skipped, rolled-back
  turns are dropped, and the turn still running is not searched.
- Search is lexical over Unicode letters and digits: whole words in
  user/assistant text count strongly, substrings of execution evidence count
  weakly. Excerpts (at most 320 bytes, markers included) come only from the
  user message or final reply; execution evidence is shown only by
  session_history_read with include_execution.
- Rollouts are loaded whole with RolloutRecorder::load_rollout_items, as
  resume and fork do, so turns and references are exact.
- Bounds: the current session is always searched first, whatever its size;
  other sessions, newest first, share a 128 MiB budget of rollout file bytes,
  checked before loading (a file that doesn't fit is skipped). At most 100
  sessions, 10,000 turns, 2 MiB of text and 20 hits. `truncated` reports when
  a bound applied.
- References are cxhr1:<session>:<turn>:<sha256>, binding the project, the
  session, the turn index and its content; reads re-check all of it. The
  scope is checked from a rollout's session meta before the rest is loaded.
- Output is JSON labelled untrusted_historical_context.
- Not offered in ephemeral sessions, except ephemeral forks, which keep
  their parent's tools and prompt cache; not offered to sub-agents or with
  remote thread stores.

Tool-list expectations and request-history snapshots now include the two
tools.

